      "body": "The spec constraint that makes this more than a cosmetic bug: RFC 8414 \u00a73.3, which the MCP spec (2026-07-28, Authorization Server Discovery) adopts as a MUST, requires the `issuer` in the metadata document to be identical to the URL the client fetched it from, and a client MUST NOT use the document otherwise. Any issuer or redirect built from the incoming `Host` header breaks as soon as a reverse proxy is in front, which is every Homarr deployment described here. The fix pattern other projects landed this week is to derive every OAuth URL from a single configured public base URL (`BASE_URL` here) and never from the request. From outside, a scan of a deployment behind a proxy shows it as an issuer mismatch, which is the same failure PagerDuty's hosted MCP server has today (pagerduty-mcp-server#169), so it is not a self-hosting-only problem.",
      "author_association": "NONE",
      "pin": null,
